    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so the salt is basically just extra data added to the end so the hash comes out different even if the password is 'password123'. where is the salt usually stored though? is it stored right next to the hash in the database or somewhere else?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are correct that the salt is extra data appended (or prepended) to the password before hashing to ensure uniqueness, even for identical users. The salt is not stored separately; it is recorded exactly next to the derived hash in the database as part of the stored record. This allows the application to add the same salt back during verification so the system can compare the new hash against the original one.\n\nStoring the salt alongside the hash is actually a security feature because it means a malicious user can see the salts but cannot reverse-engineer the passwords, thanks to the one-way nature of hashing. If the salt were hidden or not stored, an attacker would have to guess it on demand, whereas seeing it allows the attacker at rest to attack the system efficiently without compromising your database immediately.",
      "source": "target",
