    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Even though the salts are visible in the database, they still drastically increase the computational cost for an attacker compared to using no salt. Instead of using a single pre-computed rainbow table to guess millions of passwords at once, the attacker must now run a separate, time-consuming brute-force or dictionary attack for every single unique salt entry individually.\n\nThis makes cracking the database linearly slower than if they had just one giant target, often reducing the success rate of automated attacks from 100% to practically zero within a realistic timeframe unless the attacker has massive resources. Therefore, salting shifts the threat model from \"instant full compromise\" to \"incremental, resource-heavy cracking.\"",
